    <subsection xml:id="subsec-">
        <title>About this course</title>

        <paragraphs>
            <title>What is PreCalculus?</title>

            <p>
                Precalculus is meant to prepare GVSU students for calculus.
                The focus is to understand how math can be used to model and
                help us better understand the world around us.
                Math is less about the right answer, and much more about
                reasoning, critical thinking, problem solving, and making
                connections.
                Important goals of this class are also to be able to work
                creatively and independently, and to collaborate and communicate
                well with others.
            </p>
        </paragraphs>

        <paragraphs>
            <title>Course Description from the Catalog</title>

            <p>
                Study of preparatory material for calculus using symbolic
                algebra and trigonometry for solving equations, representing
                functions, and modeling, plus appropriate technology.
                Core topics: concept of function, average rate of change of a
                function, inverse and composite functions, trigonometric
                functions, exponential and logarithmic functions, and right
                triangle trigonometry.
            </p>
        </paragraphs>

        <paragraphs>
            <title>Course Objectives</title>

            <p>
                After successful completion of this course, you will be able to…
                <ol>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Demonstrate broad algebraic and trigonometric skills
                            for manipulations expressions, solving equations,
                            and understanding and representing functions, along
                            with graphical and numerical perspectives that
                            support analytical work.
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Identify the average rate of change of a function
                            and be able to compute and interpret it, with units,
                            in a wide range of settings.
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Demonstrate communication and problem-solving skills
                            that enable you to meaningfully investigate,
                            discuss, and solve authentic problems using proper
                            language, syntax, and notation, both orally and in
                            writing.
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Demonstrate understanding of the concept of a
                            function, and use functions to build models of
                            real-world quantitative phenomena.
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Use a variety of computing technology as tools for
                            making sense of mathematical concepts, creating and
                            exploring models, and developing communications
                            skills.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </paragraphs>

        <paragraphs>
            <title>Workload</title>

            <p>
                This is a five-credit class, which carries an expectation of
                roughly 10 hours of work each week outside of class.
                This course also aims to prepare you for calculus, and will help
                you hone skills such as critical thinking, problem solving,
                communication of complex ideas, or working with others.
                It is more important than ever to be able to solve non-routine
                problems and be confident in learning new ideas.
                If you work to make sense of ideas, you will be able to think
                creatively in new ways.
            </p>

            <p>
                You should therefore expect to work hard for this course.
                <em>Please do not allow yourself to fall behind</em> —you will
                find it much more difficult to catch up than to stay on top of
                the material.
                I have high expectations of you in this course and will provide
                a lot of support to help you realize them.
            </p>

            <p>
                Questions are always encouraged, never judged, and always
                answered to the best of abilities.
            </p>
        </paragraphs>
    </subsection>
    <!-- Other subsections for larger dividers -->
    <subsection xml:id="sec-assessments">
        <title>Assessments and Grades</title>

        <paragraphs>
            <title>The overall grades</title>

            <p>
                Assigning grades is the probably the most frustrating aspect of
                teaching.
                Each of you is a unique learner who brings a different
                perspective and background into our course.
                It’s therefore important to build some flexibility into any
                grading scheme.
                Given below is the percentage required to obtain a particular
                grade.
                However, I reserve the right to raise your grade if I see that
                you are working diligently and that you grow intellectually
                during the semester.
            </p>

            <tabular>
                <row bottom="minor">
                    <cell>A</cell>
                    <cell>A-</cell>
                    <cell>B+</cell>
                    <cell>B</cell>
                    <cell>B-</cell>
                    <cell>C+</cell>
                    <cell>C</cell>
                    <cell>C-</cell>
                    <cell>D+</cell>
                    <cell>D</cell>
                    <cell>F</cell>
                </row>

                <row>
                    <cell>93%</cell>
                    <cell>90%</cell>
                    <cell>87%</cell>
                    <cell>83%</cell>
                    <cell>80%</cell>
                    <cell>77%</cell>
                    <cell>73%</cell>
                    <cell>70%</cell>
                    <cell>67%</cell>
                    <cell>63%</cell>
                    <cell><m>\lt</m> 63%</cell>
                </row>
            </tabular>
        </paragraphs>

        <paragraphs>
            <title>Determination of Grades</title>

            <p>
                Your grade in this class will be determined in the following
                way:
                <dl>
                    <li>
                        <title>Daily Prep (DP) - 5%</title>

                        <p>
                            There will be an assignment due before class on
                            Tuesdays and Thursdays that is meant to get you
                            thinking about the content.
                            They will be accessible in Desmos, and the questions
                            will be graded based on effort and participation,
                            not correctness.
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <title>Attendance (A) - 5%</title>

                        <p>
                            I will take attendance in this class and it will
                            count towards your grade.
                            You can make up up to four missed classes by
                            following the instructions above in the section
                            "Attendance and making up attendance."
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <title>Online Homework or WeBWorK (WW) - 10%</title>

                        <p>
                            WeBWork is an online homework platform.
                            There will be a WeBWork set each week consisting of
                            10 exercises per set, relating to the content of the
                            course.
                            Each exercise is worth 1 point, and so each set will
                            be worth 10 points.
                            For each problem, you will have 8 attempts to
                            answer; before you reach your 8th try, come and talk
                            to me!
                            Your lowest WeBWork score will be dropped.
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <title>Written Homework (HW) - 24%</title>

                        <p>
                            Homework problems are more extended and involved
                            multi-part problems to solve that involve a formal
                            writeup.
                            You are expected to give solutions, not just
                            answers.
                            Each problem is marked on a scale of 10 points, and
                            there will be approximately 1 per week.
                            You can resubmit up to 2 problems per week on
                            problems that were submitted on time (or with a
                            deadline extension) and completed with effort.
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            Please see the "Written work expectations" section below.
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <title>Targets (T) - 36%</title>

                        <p>
                            Each week Monday in class you’ll complete a weekly
                            checkpoint to assess your understanding of up to 4
                            CORE learning targets in the course.
                            The 12 learning targets are outlined below in the
                            next section.
                            Each target receives “Excellent,” “Satisfactory,” or
                            “Not Yet.”
                        </p>

                        <p>
                            You’ll have two attempts at each of the 12 CORE
                            learning targets in class on Mondays over two
                            consecutive checkpoints.
                            If you earn “Excellent” target 1 on the one
                            checkpoint, you do not need to answer that target’s
                            question on the next checkpoint, but if you receive
                            a “Satisfactory” or “Not Yet”, you have one more
                            checkpoint to earn an “Excellent” mark.
                            If you need a third attempt on a target, you need to
                            come to see me within two weeks of getting back the
                            feedback.
                        </p>

                        <p>
                            <ul>
                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        “E” = “Excellent”: you get full credit
                                        for this target, 1 point out of 1 point,
                                        so each “E” target is worth 3% of your
                                        total grade.
                                    </p>
                                </li>

                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        “S” = “Satisfactory”: you are very close
                                        to an “Excellent”, but there is
                                        something that still can be improved,
                                        0.75 points out of 1 point.
                                    </p>
                                </li>

                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        “NY” = “Not Yet”: you don’t quite have
                                        it yet, but have opportunities to try
                                        again, 0 points out of 1 point.
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                            </ul>
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <title>Final Exam - 20%</title>

                        <p>
                            The final exam will consist of questions that
                            address big-picture ideas in the course.
                            More details to come.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </dl>
            </p>
        </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
        <title>CORE Learning Targets</title>
        <p>
            The following are the 12 Core Learning Targets for this semester. The successful demonstration of Excellence (E) on one of these targets is worth 3% of your semester grade, therefore Excellence on all the targets is worth 36% of your grade. You will have up to 2 attempts at each of these targets during your weekly checkpoints, where each week up to 4 targets may appear. You may have an additional attempt during office hours, or during an appointment with Dr. Bailey. Once you have demonstrated Excellence for a specific target on a weekly checkpoint, you will no longer need to answer the question related to that target on the other checkpoints. 
        <ol>
            <li>
                <p>
                            I can use function notation, find and simplify the output of a function given an input, and find an input that produces a given output; I can do this for functions given by a formula, a table, and a graph.

                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    I can find the average rate of change in a function on a given interval and state the units and interpret the meaning of the average rate of change in applied contexts.

                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    I can find an equation for any linear function in point-slope and slope-intercept form, given appropriate data or information.

                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    I can find and simplify values of the composition of two functions.

                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    I can determine whether a function has an inverse and find values of the inverse given input-output pairs of the original.

                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    I can generate an accurate, labeled graph of a circular function given appropriate information about the circle being traversed, and describe key features of the function.

                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    I can find the amplitude, period, and midline of a transformed version of the basic sine or cosine function; and I can find the formulas for transformed versions of the basic sine or cosine function that have certain described properties.

                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Given two points or an appropriate collection of features, I can find a formula for an exponential function that has those characteristics.

                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    I can identify the intervals where a function is increasing, decreasing, concave down, or concave up, and I can provide examples of familiar functions that have given increasing / decreasing and concave down / concave up behaviors.

                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    I can find exact values for exponential models and exact values of solutions to equations involving exponential and logarithmic expressions in contextual settings.

                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Given a right triangle with partial information about its sides and angles, I can determine the missing information for the remaining sides and angles, even if the quantities involve an unknown variable.

                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    I can identify the degree, coefficients, constant and linear terms, and leading coefficient of a polynomial, and identify zeroes, the multiplicity of zeroes, and turning points of a polynomial given its graph.

                </p>
            </li>
        </ol>

        </p>
        
    </paragraphs>

        <paragraphs>
            <title>Written work expectations</title>

            <p>
                One important part of mathematics is its emphasis on the clear
                and careful presentation of reasoning.
                This includes clearly stating the problem, making important
                observations in complete sentences, writing additional thoughts
                to clarify symbolic expressions, and showing a clear overall
                progression from problem to solution.
                The quality of your communication will count in all of your
                graded work.
                You will generally be marked on the methods used to solve a
                problem rather than just the final answer; answers alone will
                receive little credit.
                Therefore, it is very important that you include all of your
                work when writing a solution.
                The clarity with which you express your thinking will be an
                important part of your grade as well.
            </p>

            <p>
                Please take advantage of the opportunity to resubmit homework
                assignments after incorporating my feedback.
                I want you to be motivated from <em>within</em>, not by a
                somewhat arbitrary grade, and I want to allow you the
                opportunity to make mistakes and learn from them.
                It is my belief that you will learn more if you are internally
                motivated and able to resubmit your work.
            </p>

            <p>
                "The goal of training a mathematician is not achieved by
                producing correct homework." ~Terry Tao.
                Terry Tao is a very famous mathematician.
                You can read his full essay for free,
                <url href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2608.16753"><pubtitle>Mathematics in the Age of AI</pubtitle></url>.
            </p>
        </paragraphs>

        <paragraphs>
            <title>Feedback or concerns</title>

            <p>
                Any thoughts, concerns, ideas are welcome, including those
                concerning either your performance or mine.
                Please let me know if there is something that is working well
                for you or if I can be doing better.
            </p>
        </paragraphs>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-Other-Information">
        <title>Other Information</title>

        <paragraphs>
            <title>Resources</title>

            <p>
                I am a resource for you, and so are your classmates.
                You may also need help with other aspects of life that go beyond
                our coursework.
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            If you have special needs because of learning,
                            physical, or other disabilities, please contact
                            <url href="https://www.gvsu.edu/accessibility">
                Student Accessibility Resources.
              </url>
                            They will help arrange accommodations; please inform
                            me of any ways I can be supportive.
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            If you have difficulty affording groceries or
                            accessing sufficient food to eat every day, or if
                            you lack a safe and stable place to live, I
                            encourage you to visit
                            <url href="https://www.gvsu.edu/basic/">
                Replenish,
              </url>
                            a food and living resource for GVSU students.
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Mental health concerns, including overwhelming
                            stress, excessive worry, difficulty participating in
                            daily activities, and changes in mood, appetite, or
                            sleep patterns can interfere with your academic
                            performance.
                            College student surveys report that roughly 1 out of
                            3 students screen positive for a current mental
                            health concern (Healthy Minds Study).
                            GVSU values your health and wellness and provides
                            services to support your mental health.
                            If you would like mental health support or are
                            concerned for another member of our campus
                            community, reach out to the
                            <url href="https://www.gvsu.edu/counsel">
                University Counseling Center
              </url>
                            for free resources, self-help options, and services.
                            Also, visit
                            <url href="https://www.gvsu.edu/studentwellness/">
                Campus Recreation &amp; Wellness
              </url>
                            for additional health and wellness programming.
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            If you are comfortable doing so, you are welcome to
                            speak with me about your circumstances so that I can
                            advocate for you and connect you with other campus
                            resources.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </p>
        </paragraphs>

